159
00:17:11.836 --> 00:17:23.220
<v Maya>Wow. So if I find a critical vulnerability on day 28 of a 30-day exclusive window, finding it is completely useless. The adversary gets the model on day 30, and my patch won't even be ready for another 12 days.</v>

160
00:17:23.604 --> 00:17:35.606
<v Daniel>The human element introduces a totally catastrophic lag. remediation takes real time. If the window doesn't account for the speed of human bureaucracy and engineering, the protective gap basically collapses. SARAH</v>

161
00:17:35.626 --> 00:17:50.456
<v Maya>BALDWIN- OK, so bringing this all together for you listening, the public debate around AI regulation is almost always presented as this really stark binary choice. We are told to either release everything openly so innovation thrives or hoard the</v>

162
00:17:50.516 --> 00:17:59.601
<v Maya>models in bunkers so the grid doesn't collapse. We are told to either regulate the industry into the ground or just let the court sort out the damages after the fact.</v>

163
00:18:00.261 --> 00:18:03.802
<v Daniel>And Joshua Gann's argument is that both of those framings are entirely wrong.</v>

164
00:18:04.063 --> 00:18:09.384
<v Maya>The real levers of power are a specific calendar date and a partial liability rate calibrated together.</v>

165
00:18:09.604 --> 00:18:10.144
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

166
00:18:10.245 --> 00:18:22.929
<v Maya>A liability rate without an exclusive window just creates a useless, wildly expensive tug of war. A window without liability means the developer has no financial incentive to pay for the massive compute needed to search for the bugs in the first place.</v>

167
00:18:22.949 --> 00:18:23.829
<v Daniel>They are compliments, yeah.</v>

168
00:18:24.029 --> 00:18:38.713
<v Maya>And that voluntary 30-day window the U.S. government suggested, it is just a round number that nobody bothered to actually calibrate to the capabilities of the AI. The math suggests the optimal window is finite and honestly likely shorter than our intuition</v>

169
00:18:38.753 --> 00:18:41.894
<v Maya>dictates because adding time doesn't proportionally add effort.</v>

170
00:18:42.014 --> 00:18:48.436
<v Daniel>Which leaves a profoundly disturbing open question for you to consider as these frontier models continue to scale up.</v>

171
00:18:48.556 --> 00:18:48.837
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

172
00:18:49.037 --> 00:19:03.857
<v Daniel>Gantz provides this incredibly elegant theory for a perfect regulatory framework. But if AI models reach a point where they can detect millions of latent vulnerabilities across our entire digital infrastructure in seconds, while human bureaucracies still take</v>

173
00:19:03.977 --> 00:19:12.423
<v Daniel>weeks to write and deploy a single patch, does the speed of human remediation ultimately render any regulatory head start completely meaningless?</v>

174
00:19:12.744 --> 00:19:20.549
<v Maya>Could we achieve perfectly calibrated protection depth on paper while suffering total systemic failure in practice? It's definitely something to keep you awake tonight.</v>

175
00:19:20.589 --> 00:19:21.230
<v Daniel>Until next time.</v>
